      "comments": [
        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12545810/comment/13226025",
          "id": "13226025",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=schans",
            "name": "schans",
            "key": "schans",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?avatarId=10452",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&avatarId=10452",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&avatarId=10452",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&avatarId=10452"
            },
            "displayName": "Bart van der Schans",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "Europe/Amsterdam"
          },
          "body": "The only problem I can think of is when running two JR instances in one JVM. If that is a concern we could add a method (in the JR api?) to fetch the id. Is this a real concern or can I go ahead and apply the patch?\n",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=schans",
            "name": "schans",
            "key": "schans",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?avatarId=10452",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&avatarId=10452",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&avatarId=10452",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&avatarId=10452"
            },
            "displayName": "Bart van der Schans",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "Europe/Amsterdam"
          },
          "created": "2012-03-09T11:31:20.098+0000",
          "updated": "2012-03-09T11:31:20.098+0000"
        },
        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12545810/comment/13226091",
          "id": "13226091",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=stillalex",
            "name": "stillalex",
            "key": "alex.parvulescu",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=alex.parvulescu&avatarId=41133",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=alex.parvulescu&avatarId=41133",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&ownerId=alex.parvulescu&avatarId=41133",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&ownerId=alex.parvulescu&avatarId=41133"
            },
            "displayName": "Alex Deparvu",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "America/Los_Angeles"
          },
          "body": ">  two JR instances in one JVM\nI can see one problem where you start 2 nodes one after the other, the first one generates an id and sets the system property, then the second one will use the already set property as the id.\nThis will break the scenario where you start 2 nodes just to run a simple test on you local machine, and it can probably affect unit testing / integration testing as well.\n\nIf this is only about convenience, probably adding a method to JctUtils would be the easiest.",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=stillalex",
            "name": "stillalex",
            "key": "alex.parvulescu",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=alex.parvulescu&avatarId=41133",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=alex.parvulescu&avatarId=41133",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&ownerId=alex.parvulescu&avatarId=41133",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&ownerId=alex.parvulescu&avatarId=41133"
            },
            "displayName": "Alex Deparvu",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "America/Los_Angeles"
          },
          "created": "2012-03-09T14:22:31.846+0000",
          "updated": "2012-03-09T14:22:31.846+0000"
        },
        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12545810/comment/13226095",
          "id": "13226095",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=jukkaz",
            "name": "jukkaz",
            "key": "jukkaz",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=jukkaz&avatarId=15130",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=jukkaz&avatarId=15130",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&ownerId=jukkaz&avatarId=15130",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&ownerId=jukkaz&avatarId=15130"
            },
            "displayName": "Jukka Zitting",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "America/New_York"
          },
          "body": "In CRX we expose the cluster node id through an extra \"crx.cluster.id\" repository descriptor. I think Jackrabbit could well do something similar.",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=jukkaz",
            "name": "jukkaz",
            "key": "jukkaz",
